00:13:32.079 --> 00:13:33.079
Right.

00:13:33.079 --> 00:13:34.079
This is a common thing.

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:35.079
And it's sort of like with eggs.

00:13:35.079 --> 00:13:36.560
Coffee is good and then it's bad and then
it's good.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.070
And it depends how you test it or you ask
or when.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.890
You know, with anything, food and drink related.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:46.709
It seems that part of the difficulty is whenever
you're adding or taking something away, you're

00:13:46.709 --> 00:13:48.610
always replacing with something else.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:53.730
And it can be hard to know whether the effect
is from the removal of some or what is the

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:58.100
best science we currently have about those
types of claims, about a couple of cups of

00:13:58.100 --> 00:13:59.410
coffee and longevity.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:05.779
Speaker 2: So what we do know is that healthy,
non-pregnant, non nursing individuals can

00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:12.870
include an energy drink or a strong cup of
coffee or two in their daily diet with in

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.380
moderation.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:18.070
So just like you would do anything else in
moderation for healthy, non-pregnant non nursing

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:19.840
individuals.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:24.830
Caffeine in moderation is thought to be generally
regarded as safe.

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:25.830
And that's what we know.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.080
Speaker 1: Now, that's extraordinarily narrow
language.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.529
It is generally regarded this.

00:14:31.529 --> 00:14:32.910
Yeah.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:39.399
You know, when when my girlfriend when we
were in the process of starting a pregnancy,

00:14:39.399 --> 00:14:48.680
she was told by by a doctor that some of the
assistive medications that exist, we don't

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:54.329
currently believe they increase the risk of
reproductive cancers, which again, when I

00:14:54.329 --> 00:14:57.460
hear that, I say it sounds like they might.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:59.829
It sounds like they might.

00:14:59.829 --> 00:15:01.060
And so we really don't know.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.080
Speaker 2: We know that it's generally regarded
as safe.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:05.080
Okay.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:10.550
That's what we know with the exceptions for
pregnancy, for nursing, for adolescents and

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.959
for individuals who might be susceptible,
whoever they are.

00:15:12.959 --> 00:15:13.959
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:15:13.959 --> 00:15:14.959
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:15:14.959 --> 00:15:18.110
Speaker 1: Well, which I guess I guess the
answer is we maybe would like to know more

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:19.740
than we actually do know right now.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:21.269
Speaker 2: It's true.

00:15:21.269 --> 00:15:25.750
Remember that there are constraints around
science that would establish what safe part

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:30.000
of it is, the individual metabolism absorption
of these substances.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.550
And then part of it is the constraints over
long term health effects that are unknown

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.360
and that limit our ability to reproduce in
the laboratory what people commonly do in

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.360
real life.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:41.360
Right.

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:45.110
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to ask you
about, do you think that so obviously with

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:51.500
the pre-mixed drinks there off the shelf,
it's a regulatory decision that that was made.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:56.800
When it comes to I mean, you tell me coffee
of different kinds or energy drinks that are

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:58.060
just energy drinks.

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:02.149
Do you think there needs to be more regulation
or government involvement or is it really

00:16:02.149 --> 00:16:06.459
just about educating people and making people
aware of the amounts of these things that

00:16:06.459 --> 00:16:07.459
they're consuming?

00:16:07.459 --> 00:16:10.000
Speaker 2: So you asked my opinion, so I'm
going to give you an opinion.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:11.000
Yes.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:15.510
And I and I think it's poppycock that they
are regulated as dietary supplements.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.010
They're they're beverages.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.529
And that was a decision made in the mid 1990s.

00:16:20.529 --> 00:16:25.199
The decision that the FDA made about limiting
the caffeine in cola beverages was made in

00:16:25.199 --> 00:16:26.740
1959.

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:32.300
And the energy drinks really came onto the
market in the 1990s, particularly late 1980s,

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:37.370
early 1990s, and in the mid 1990s, the FDA
made a decision that said these are dietary

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:39.899
supplements, they're they're not beverages.

00:16:39.899 --> 00:16:44.509
And if they were regulated in the same way
as are the can of Coca Cola that you can buy

00:16:44.509 --> 00:16:48.910
in the grocery store right next to them, then
that would make more sense to me.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:49.910
I think it's poppycock.

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:55.370
Speaker 1: And if they were regulated in the
same way as soda, the difference is would

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:58.690
be labeling or also there would be limits
that would be placed.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:00.339
Speaker 2: Yeah, there would be limits.

00:17:00.339 --> 00:17:02.269
So we mentioned guarana and your mama.

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:04.620
Those are both natural sources of caffeine.

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:07.520
And so they are listed as additives to energy
drinks.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:08.850
They're more caffeine.

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:13.490
Now, I did a little research to prepare for
speaking with you and looked at a couple of

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:14.490
things on the shelf.

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:17.900
And most of them most of the beverages that
I saw do list the amount of caffeine that's

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:18.900
in them.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:19.900
Okay.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.850
However, there is not.

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:25.949
I just think we would not you would not give,
you know, three or four cups of coffee to

00:17:25.949 --> 00:17:27.179
your average 13 year old.

00:17:27.179 --> 00:17:31.590
You would not let a kid in high school think
that an energy drink could serve as a sports

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.770
drink when they're not.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:34.770
You would.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:36.200
And yet that's what goes on.

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:38.200
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:39.200
Wow.

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.190
Well, we will see if that ever.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.340
Is there an appetite to change that, do you
think?

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:44.340
It doesn't seem like it.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.340
Or there is.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:46.340
Oh, okay.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:47.340
Speaker 2: There is.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.120
And letters have been written by a couple
of senators.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:50.120
And these.

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:51.120
This goes back a while, though.

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:52.120
Yeah.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:53.120
Is there a current appetite?

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:54.120
No.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.880
Was there a lot of appetite in the wake of
the research about the health effects of energy

00:17:57.880 --> 00:17:58.950
drinks on alcohol?

00:17:58.950 --> 00:17:59.950
Yes.

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:06.070
Did that did that stir the pot about the health
effects of energy drinks at all without alcohol?

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.070
Yes.

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:11.429
Has that energy and enthusiasm for making
change continued?

00:18:11.429 --> 00:18:14.679
It has not over the last decade, and I'm not
sure why that is.

00:18:14.679 --> 00:18:16.390
Speaker 1: Understood.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:17.390
Understood.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:22.169
We've been speaking with Dr. Mary Claire O'Brien,
professor of emergency medicine at Wake Forest

00:18:22.169 --> 00:18:23.679
University School of Medicine.

00:18:23.679 --> 00:18:24.920
Thank you so much for your time.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:25.920
Appreciate your insights.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:27.039
Speaker 2: You're welcome.

00:18:27.039 --> 00:18:31.480
I am impressed with your level of knowledge
about the science of the subject, and you

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:32.480
can call me any time.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:33.510
Speaker 1: Thank you.

